===== Available Tools =====
- ToolName: list_schemas             Description: List all schemas in the database
- ToolName: list_objects             Description: List objects in a schema
- ToolName: get_object_details       Description: Show detailed information about a database object
- ToolName: explain_query            Description: Explains the execution plan for a SQL query, showing how the database will execute it and provides detailed cost estimates.
- ToolName: analyze_workload_indexes Description: Analyze frequently executed queries in the database and recommend optimal indexes
- ToolName: analyze_query_indexes    Description: Analyze a list of (up to 10) SQL queries and recommend optimal indexes
- ToolName: analyze_db_health        Description: Analyzes database health. Here are the available health checks:
- index - checks for invalid, duplicate, and bloated indexes
- connection - checks the number of connection and their utilization
- vacuum - checks vacuum health for transaction id wraparound
- sequence - checks sequences at risk of exceeding their maximum value
- replication - checks replication health including lag and slots
- buffer - checks for buffer cache hit rates for indexes and tables
- constraint - checks for invalid constraints
- all - runs all checks
You can optionally specify a single health check or a comma-separated list of health checks. The default is 'all' checks.
- ToolName: get_top_queries          Description: Reports the slowest or most resource-intensive queries using data from the 'pg_stat_statements' extension.
- ToolName: execute_sql              Description: Execute any SQL query

===== Execution Logs =====
| explain_query {"sql": "SELECT \n    c.customer_id,\n    c.first_name,\n    c.last_name,\n    c.email,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT p.payment_id) as total_payments,\n    SUM(p.amount) as total_spent,\n    AVG(p.amount) as avg_payment,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT EXTRACT(month FROM p.payment_date)) as active_months,\n    MAX(p.payment_date) as last_payment,\n    MIN(p.payment_date) as first_payment,\n    (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM payment p2 WHERE p2.customer_id = c.customer_id AND p2.amount > 5.0) as high_value_payments,\n    (SELECT SUM(amount) FROM payment p3 WHERE p3.customer_id = c.customer_id AND p3.payment_date >= '2007-03-01') as recent_spending\nFROM customer c\nJOIN payment p ON c.customer_id = p.customer_id\nWHERE c.active = 1\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name, c.last_name, c.email\nHAVING COUNT(p.payment_id) >= 10\nORDER BY total_spent DESC, total_payments DESC;","analyze": true}
The explain analyze output reveals the following performance characteristics:

- Total execution time is around 1314 ms.
- The main cost is in the Aggregate and Sort operations which process 14,227 rows.
- The Join between customer and payment on customer_id is efficient (via index scan and merge join).
- However, the two correlated subqueries inside the SELECT (for high_value_payments and recent_spending) each cause a sequential scan on the payment table per customer, resulting in repeated scans of payment rows (loops: 583).
- These repeated subqueries inside the aggregation are likely the main bottleneck.

Optimization approach:

1. Replace correlated subqueries with conditional aggregation in the main query to avoid repeated scans:
   - COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE amount > 5) AS high_value_payments
   - SUM(amount) FILTER (WHERE payment_date >= '2007-03-01') AS recent_spending
2. Ensure proper indexes on payment(customer_id, amount) and payment(customer_id, payment_date) exist to speed filtering.
3. Verify the indexes and table statistics are up to date.

I will now rewrite the query with conditional aggregation and check for indexes on payment covering customer_id, amount, and payment_date columns.
| execute_sql {"sql": "-- Check existing indexes on payment table\nSELECT tablename, indexname, indexdef FROM pg_indexes WHERE tablename = 'payment';"}
| execute_sql {"sql": "-- Rewrite query with conditional aggregation\nSELECT \n    c.customer_id,\n    c.first_name,\n    c.last_name,\n    c.email,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT p.payment_id) as total_payments,\n    SUM(p.amount) as total_spent,\n    AVG(p.amount) as avg_payment,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT EXTRACT(month FROM p.payment_date)) as active_months,\n    MAX(p.payment_date) as last_payment,\n    MIN(p.payment_date) as first_payment,\n    COUNT(p.payment_id) FILTER (WHERE p.amount > 5.0) as high_value_payments,\n    SUM(p.amount) FILTER (WHERE p.payment_date >= '2007-03-01') as recent_spending\nFROM customer c\nJOIN payment p ON c.customer_id = p.customer_id\nWHERE c.active = 1\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name, c.last_name, c.email\nHAVING COUNT(p.payment_id) >= 10\nORDER BY total_spent DESC, total_payments DESC;"}
| explain_query {"sql": "SELECT \n    c.customer_id,\n    c.first_name,\n    c.last_name,\n    c.email,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT p.payment_id) as total_payments,\n    SUM(p.amount) as total_spent,\n    AVG(p.amount) as avg_payment,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT EXTRACT(month FROM p.payment_date)) as active_months,\n    MAX(p.payment_date) as last_payment,\n    MIN(p.payment_date) as first_payment,\n    COUNT(p.payment_id) FILTER (WHERE p.amount > 5.0) as high_value_payments,\n    SUM(p.amount) FILTER (WHERE p.payment_date >= '2007-03-01') as recent_spending\nFROM customer c\nJOIN payment p ON c.customer_id = p.customer_id\nWHERE c.active = 1\nGROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name, c.last_name, c.email\nHAVING COUNT(p.payment_id) >= 10\nORDER BY total_spent DESC, total_payments DESC;","analyze": true}
Optimization approach and results:

1. Rewrote the query to replace the correlated subqueries in SELECT with conditional aggregation using FILTER clause. This eliminates repeated scans over the payment table per customer.
2. Checked existing indexes on payment table; none directly cover customer_id with amount or payment_date filters. Consider adding indexes on (customer_id, amount) and (customer_id, payment_date) for further improvements.
3. Re-ran EXPLAIN ANALYZE on the optimized query.

Performance improvement:
- Original query execution time: ~1314 ms
- Optimized query execution time: ~15 ms

This is roughly an 87x improvement in execution time by removing correlated subqueries and replacing with conditional aggregation.

Final recommended optimized query:

```sql
SELECT 
    c.customer_id,
    c.first_name,
    c.last_name,
    c.email,
    COUNT(DISTINCT p.payment_id) as total_payments,
    SUM(p.amount) as total_spent,
    AVG(p.amount) as avg_payment,
    COUNT(DISTINCT EXTRACT(month FROM p.payment_date)) as active_months,
    MAX(p.payment_date) as last_payment,
    MIN(p.payment_date) as first_payment,
    COUNT(p.payment_id) FILTER (WHERE p.amount > 5.0) as high_value_payments,
    SUM(p.amount) FILTER (WHERE p.payment_date >= '2007-03-01') as recent_spending
FROM customer c
JOIN payment p ON c.customer_id = p.customer_id
WHERE c.active = 1
GROUP BY c.customer_id, c.first_name, c.last_name, c.email
HAVING COUNT(p.payment_id) >= 10
ORDER BY total_spent DESC, total_payments DESC;
```

Additional suggestions:
- Add indexes on payment (customer_id, amount) and (customer_id, payment_date) to help in filtering conditions.
- Regularly analyze and vacuum tables to keep statistics up to date.

